id: k8s.controller
kind: kubernetes
title: Controller
aliases: controller, operator, control loop
tags: architecture, controllers
questions:
  - what is the difference between a controller and an operator?
invariant: A controller is a reconciliation loop over a resource type; an operator is a controller that packages domain operational knowledge for one application.
answer_30s: Every controller watches at least one resource, diffs desired against observed state and acts; an operator is a controller plus the domain knowledge (upgrades, backups, failover) for a specific system.
answer_2m: |
  The implementation shape is the same: informer/watch plus workqueue, a
  reconcile function keyed by object identity, status writes to report
  progress, and rate-limited requeue for retries. Built-in controllers own the
  built-in kinds (Deployment, StatefulSet, Job, endpoints); custom controllers
  own CRDs. What separates a good controller from a script is the same pair as
  above: idempotence and level-triggered logic. Ownership references
  (ownerReferences) are how garbage collection knows that deleting the parent
  should delete the children -- which is also why a StatefulSet's PVCs survive:
  they are not owned by it.
when_to_use:
  - encoding operational knowledge as code instead of runbooks
commands:
  - kubectl get events --field-selector involvedObject.kind=MyResource => what a custom controller decided
failure_modes:
  - fighting another controller over the same object (two writers, no single owner)
  - not writing status, so failures are invisible to operators
related:
  - k8s.reconciliation
  - k8s.desired-state
  - k8s.api-server
  - recipe.controller
sources:
  - https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/architecture/controller/
